<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lahn is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 245 km long and a right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It rises in the Rothaar Mountains and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Giessen</a>, Wetzlar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a>, Limburg and Nassau to join the Rhine at Lahnstein. Its valley was a heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a> and other Hessian counties, and its iron ore supported mining and smelting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahn.jpg" alt="Lahn" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Verlaufskarte_Lahn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
